result:
Fixed in `tokio/src/sync/batch_semaphore.rs:376`.

**Cause:** the closed flag lives in the low bit of the same `AtomicUsize` as the permit count (`CLOSED = 1`, `PERMIT_SHIFT = 1`). `forget_permits` read the whole word, shifted out the permit count, and CAS'd back `new << PERMIT_SHIFT` — writing a zero into the flag bit. Any non-zero `forget_permits` on a closed semaphore therefore cleared `CLOSED`, so `is_closed()` went false and `try_acquire`/`poll_acquire` began handing out permits again. (`release`/`add_permits` uses `fetch_add`, and the acquire paths bail out early when closed, so this was the only site that clobbered the flag.)

**Fix:** preserve the flag bit in the CAS:

```rust
let new = curr.saturating_sub(n);
// Preserve the flag bits stored alongside the permit count, so
// that forgetting permits does not reopen a closed semaphore.
let new_bits = (new << Self::PERMIT_SHIFT) | (curr_bits & Self::CLOSED);
match self
    .permits
    .compare_exchange_weak(curr_bits, new_bits, AcqRel, Acquire)
```

**Verification:** `forget_closed` in `tokio/tests/sync_semaphore.rs` fails on the unpatched tree (`assertion failed: sem.is_closed()`) and passes with the fix. Full runs of `sync_semaphore`, `sync_semaphore_owned`, `sync_mutex`, `sync_rwlock`, and the tokio lib unit tests pass (189 tests, 0 failures).
